{
  "dom_id": "step-q143142",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is proportional to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis. The derivative at the point $(-3,6)$ equals $12.$ What differential equation describes this curve?",
  "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **proportional** to the **shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis* is given by $|y|.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *proportional to* the shortest distance from $P$ to the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\cdot |y|\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k|y|.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{′}(x)=12$ when at the point $(-3,6).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12=k|6|\\,⟹\\,k=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=2|y|.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
